WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.396 --> 00:24.876 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference February 1,

00:26.136 --> 00:54.326 align:middle
2023 CHAIR POWELL.

00:54.976 --> 01:10.526 align:middle
Good afternoon, and welcome.

01:13.536 --> 01:58.596 align:middle
My colleagues and I
understand the hardship

02:51.736 --> 03:00.256 align:middle
that high inflation is causing,
and we are strongly committed

03:00.446 --> 03:03.136 align:middle
to bringing inflation back
down to our 2 percent goal.

03:04.636 --> 03:07.216 align:middle
Over the past year, we
have taken forceful actions

03:07.216 --> 03:08.956 align:middle
to tighten the stance
of monetary policy.

03:09.336 --> 03:12.126 align:middle
We have covered a lot of
ground, and the full effects

03:12.126 --> 03:14.686 align:middle
of our rapid tightening
so far are yet to be felt.

03:15.756 --> 03:17.946 align:middle
Even so, we have
more work to do.

03:19.436 --> 03:21.166 align:middle
Price stability is
the responsibility

03:21.166 --> 03:22.866 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve
and serves

03:22.866 --> 03:24.346 align:middle
as the bedrock of our economy.

03:25.326 --> 03:26.376 align:middle
Without price stability,

03:26.476 --> 03:28.336 align:middle
the economy does
not work for anyone.

03:29.216 --> 03:31.086 align:middle
In particular, without
price stability,

03:31.326 --> 03:33.846 align:middle
we will not achieve
a sustained period

03:33.846 --> 03:36.026 align:middle
of labor market conditions
that benefit all.

03:38.386 --> 03:41.686 align:middle
Today, the FOMC raised
our policy interest rate

03:41.686 --> 03:43.086 align:middle
by 25 basis points.

03:43.996 --> 03:45.196 align:middle
We continue to anticipate

03:45.196 --> 03:48.356 align:middle
that ongoing increases will
be appropriate in order

03:48.356 --> 03:50.236 align:middle
to attain a stance
of monetary policy

03:50.236 --> 03:53.276 align:middle
that is sufficiently
restrictive to return inflation

03:53.276 --> 03:54.606 align:middle
to 2 percent over time.

03:55.626 --> 03:58.266 align:middle
In addition, we are
continuing the process

03:58.266 --> 04:00.966 align:middle
of significantly reducing the
size of our balance sheet.

04:02.516 --> 04:05.146 align:middle
Restoring price stability will
likely require maintaining a

04:05.146 --> 04:07.216 align:middle
restrictive stance
for some time.

04:07.936 --> 04:11.526 align:middle
I will have more to say about
today's monetary policy actions

04:11.526 --> 04:14.076 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

04:15.916 --> 04:19.026 align:middle
The U.S. economy slowed
significantly last year,

04:19.196 --> 04:23.066 align:middle
with real GDP rising at a
below-trend pace of 1 percent.

04:24.386 --> 04:26.936 align:middle
Recent indicators point to
modest growth of spending

04:26.936 --> 04:28.056 align:middle
and production this quarter.

04:28.976 --> 04:32.066 align:middle
Consumer spending appears to
be expanding at a subdued pace,

04:32.256 --> 04:34.486 align:middle
in part reflecting tighter
financial conditions

04:34.486 --> 04:35.466 align:middle
over the past year.

04:36.936 --> 04:39.316 align:middle
Activity in the housing
sector continues to weaken,

04:39.456 --> 04:41.726 align:middle
largely reflecting
higher mortgage rates.

04:43.116 --> 04:46.016 align:middle
Higher interest rates and
slower output growth also appear

04:46.016 --> 04:48.386 align:middle
to be weighing on
business fixed investment.

04:51.016 --> 04:52.316 align:middle
Despite the slowdown in growth,

04:52.366 --> 04:54.476 align:middle
the labor market
remains extremely tight,

04:54.776 --> 04:56.926 align:middle
with the unemployment
rate at a 50-year low,

04:57.706 --> 05:01.386 align:middle
job vacancies still very high,
and wage growth elevated.

05:02.716 --> 05:05.886 align:middle
Job gains have been robust, with
employment rising by an average

05:05.886 --> 05:09.666 align:middle
of 247,000 jobs per month
over the last three months.

05:11.236 --> 05:14.026 align:middle
Although the pace of job gains
has slowed over the course

05:14.026 --> 05:17.256 align:middle
of the past year and nominal
wage growth has shown some signs

05:17.256 --> 05:20.536 align:middle
of easing, the labor market
continues to be out of balance.

05:21.446 --> 05:24.546 align:middle
Labor demand substantially
exceeds the supply

05:24.546 --> 05:28.306 align:middle
of available workers, and the
labor force participation rate

05:28.306 --> 05:32.066 align:middle
has changed little
from a year ago.

05:32.446 --> 05:35.906 align:middle
Inflation remains well above our
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

05:36.256 --> 05:38.176 align:middle
Over the 12 months
ending in December,

05:38.726 --> 05:41.946 align:middle
total PCE prices
rose 5.0 percent;

05:42.916 --> 05:45.806 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

05:46.166 --> 05:48.846 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
4.4 percent.

05:49.976 --> 05:51.136 align:middle
The inflation data received

05:51.136 --> 05:54.166 align:middle
over the past three months
show a welcome reduction

05:54.166 --> 05:55.746 align:middle
in the monthly pace
of increases.

05:57.136 --> 05:58.996 align:middle
And, while recent
developments are encouraging,

05:59.726 --> 06:02.396 align:middle
we will need substantially
more evidence to be confident

06:02.706 --> 06:05.136 align:middle
that inflation is on a
sustained downward path.

06:07.166 --> 06:08.606 align:middle
Despite elevated inflation,

06:08.906 --> 06:11.076 align:middle
longer-term inflation
expectations appear

06:11.076 --> 06:14.726 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

06:14.726 --> 06:17.416 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters,

06:18.046 --> 06:20.526 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

06:21.726 --> 06:23.626 align:middle
But that's not grounds
for complacency.

06:24.646 --> 06:27.706 align:middle
Although inflation has moderated
recently, it remains too high.

06:28.816 --> 06:31.466 align:middle
The longer the current bout
of high inflation continues,

06:31.986 --> 06:33.716 align:middle
the greater the chance
that expectations

06:33.716 --> 06:36.196 align:middle
of higher inflation
will become entrenched.

06:38.516 --> 06:40.906 align:middle
The Fed's monetary
policy actions are guided

06:40.906 --> 06:43.376 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

06:43.376 --> 06:45.296 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

06:46.276 --> 06:48.036 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

06:48.196 --> 06:50.746 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

06:51.106 --> 06:55.356 align:middle
as it erodes purchasing power,
especially for those least able

06:55.356 --> 06:58.236 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

06:58.556 --> 06:59.826 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

07:01.446 --> 07:03.926 align:middle
We are highly attentive to
the risks that inflation poses

07:03.926 --> 07:07.296 align:middle
to both sides of our mandate,
and we are strongly committed

07:07.646 --> 07:10.226 align:middle
to returning inflation to
our 2 percent objective.

07:12.396 --> 07:13.456 align:middle
At today's meeting,

07:13.456 --> 07:15.906 align:middle
the Committee raised the target
range for the federal funds rate

07:15.906 --> 07:18.826 align:middle
by 25 basis points,
bringing the target range

07:18.826 --> 07:21.006 align:middle
to 4½ to 4  percent.

07:21.746 --> 07:23.766 align:middle
And we are continuing
the process

07:23.766 --> 07:26.316 align:middle
of significantly reducing the
size of our balance sheet.

07:27.796 --> 07:30.156 align:middle
With today's action, we
have raised interest rates

07:30.156 --> 07:32.786 align:middle
by 4½ percentage points
over the past year.

07:33.816 --> 07:36.226 align:middle
We continue to anticipate
that ongoing increases

07:36.226 --> 07:38.306 align:middle
in the target range for the
federal funds rate will be

07:38.306 --> 07:41.586 align:middle
appropriate in order to attain
a stance of monetary policy

07:41.586 --> 07:44.406 align:middle
that is sufficiently
restrictive to return inflation

07:44.406 --> 07:45.736 align:middle
to 2 percent over time.

07:47.576 --> 07:50.096 align:middle
We are seeing the effects of
our policy actions on demand

07:50.376 --> 07:51.936 align:middle
in the most interest-sensitive
sectors

07:51.936 --> 07:53.526 align:middle
of the economy, particularly
housing.

07:55.026 --> 07:57.186 align:middle
It will take time, however,
for the full effects

07:57.246 --> 07:59.046 align:middle
of monetary restraint
to be realized,

07:59.276 --> 08:00.566 align:middle
especially on inflation.

08:02.016 --> 08:04.646 align:middle
In light of the cumulative
tightening of monetary policy

08:04.936 --> 08:08.366 align:middle
and the lags with which monetary
policy affects economic activity

08:08.366 --> 08:11.586 align:middle
and inflation, the Committee
decided to raise interest rates

08:11.636 --> 08:15.346 align:middle
by 25 basis points today,
continuing the step-down

08:15.346 --> 08:17.886 align:middle
from last year's rapid
pace of increases.

08:19.186 --> 08:21.906 align:middle
Shifting to a slower pace will
better allow the Committee

08:21.906 --> 08:24.426 align:middle
to assess the economy's
progress toward our goals

08:24.906 --> 08:27.216 align:middle
as we determine the
extent of future increases

08:27.246 --> 08:29.626 align:middle
that will be required
to attain a sufficiently

08:29.626 --> 08:30.686 align:middle
restrictive stance.

08:32.306 --> 08:34.476 align:middle
We will continue to make our
decisions meeting by meeting,

08:34.476 --> 08:37.446 align:middle
taking into account the
totality of incoming data

08:37.446 --> 08:39.336 align:middle
and their implications
for the outlook

08:39.336 --> 08:41.256 align:middle
for economic activity
and inflation.

08:43.366 --> 08:45.846 align:middle
We have been taking forceful
steps to moderate demand

08:45.936 --> 08:48.226 align:middle
so that it comes into better
alignment with supply.

08:49.156 --> 08:51.706 align:middle
Our overarching focus
is using our tools

08:51.706 --> 08:54.246 align:middle
to bring inflation back
down to our 2 percent goal

08:55.116 --> 08:57.526 align:middle
and to keep longer-term
inflation expectations

08:57.756 --> 08:58.336 align:middle
well anchored.

09:00.076 --> 09:02.536 align:middle
Reducing inflation is
likely to require a period

09:02.536 --> 09:04.526 align:middle
of below-trend growth
and some softening

09:04.526 --> 09:05.756 align:middle
of labor market conditions.

09:07.086 --> 09:10.276 align:middle
Restoring price stability is
essential to set the stage

09:10.276 --> 09:11.996 align:middle
for achieving maximum employment

09:11.996 --> 09:13.896 align:middle
and stable prices
over the longer run.

09:15.416 --> 09:17.886 align:middle
The historical record
cautions strongly

09:17.886 --> 09:19.916 align:middle
against prematurely
loosening policy.

09:20.996 --> 09:23.226 align:middle
We will stay the course,
until the job is done.

09:24.846 --> 09:28.296 align:middle
To conclude, we understand that
our actions affect communities,

09:28.716 --> 09:30.646 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

09:31.386 --> 09:33.896 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

09:34.856 --> 09:36.706 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

09:36.766 --> 09:38.576 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

09:38.576 --> 09:39.966 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

09:40.966 --> 09:42.816 align:middle
Thank you, and I look
forward to your questions.

09:43.796 --> 09:45.716 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:47.426 --> 09:49.286 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

09:49.466 --> 09:51.386 align:middle
Chris Rugaber, the
Associated Press.

09:51.386 --> 09:52.506 align:middle
Thank you for doing this.

09:53.146 --> 09:55.576 align:middle
As you know, financial
conditions have loosened

09:55.726 --> 10:00.016 align:middle
since the fall with bond yields
falling, which has also brought

10:00.016 --> 10:03.366 align:middle
down mortgage rates, and the
stock market posted a solid gain

10:03.366 --> 10:04.016 align:middle
in January.

10:04.586 --> 10:07.316 align:middle
Does that make your job of
combating inflation harder?

10:07.406 --> 10:11.476 align:middle
And could you see lifting rates
higher than you otherwise would

10:11.506 --> 10:14.486 align:middle
to offset the increase
in-or to offset the easing

10:14.486 --> 10:15.436 align:middle
of financial conditions?

10:15.646 --> 10:16.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:16.796 --> 10:19.926 align:middle
So it is important that overall
financial conditions continue

10:19.926 --> 10:22.376 align:middle
to reflect the policy restraint
that we're putting in place

10:22.806 --> 10:24.476 align:middle
in order to bring
inflation down to 2 percent.

10:25.136 --> 10:27.566 align:middle
And, of course, financial
conditions have tightened very

10:27.566 --> 10:28.976 align:middle
significantly over
the past year.

10:29.956 --> 10:32.626 align:middle
I would say that our focus
is not on short-term moves

10:32.626 --> 10:35.076 align:middle
but on sustained changes to
broader financial conditions.

10:35.596 --> 10:37.146 align:middle
And it is our judgment
that we're not

10:37.146 --> 10:39.726 align:middle
yet at a sufficiently
restrictive policy stance,

10:40.436 --> 10:43.566 align:middle
which is why we say that we
expect ongoing hikes will

10:43.566 --> 10:44.156 align:middle
be appropriate.

10:45.036 --> 10:47.976 align:middle
Of course, many things affect
financial conditions-not just

10:47.976 --> 10:48.506 align:middle
our policy.

10:49.546 --> 10:52.316 align:middle
And we will take into account
overall financial conditions

10:52.316 --> 10:54.336 align:middle
along with many other
factors as we set policy.

10:54.336 --> 10:54.816 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:56.886 --> 11:00.296 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

11:01.196 --> 11:03.346 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell, thank you
for taking our questions.

11:03.506 --> 11:04.916 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

11:05.706 --> 11:09.196 align:middle
Over the last quarter, we've
seen a deceleration in prices,

11:09.196 --> 11:12.386 align:middle
in wages, and a fall
in consumer spending,

11:12.816 --> 11:14.646 align:middle
all while the unemployment
rate has been able

11:14.646 --> 11:16.136 align:middle
to stay at a historic low.

11:16.716 --> 11:18.236 align:middle
Does this, at all,
change your view

11:18.376 --> 11:20.476 align:middle
of how much the unemployment
rate would need to go up,

11:20.796 --> 11:22.316 align:middle
if at all, to see inflation come

11:22.316 --> 11:23.536 align:middle
down to the levels
you're looking for?

11:23.786 --> 11:24.276 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:25.006 --> 11:29.286 align:middle
So I would say it is a good
thing that the disinflation

11:29.286 --> 11:33.806 align:middle
that we have seen so far
has not come at the expense

11:33.806 --> 11:35.276 align:middle
of a weaker labor market.

11:35.976 --> 11:38.886 align:middle
But I would also say that
that disinflationary process

11:38.886 --> 11:42.386 align:middle
that you now see under way
is really at an early stage.

11:42.906 --> 11:44.436 align:middle
What you see is, really,

11:44.436 --> 11:48.726 align:middle
in the goods sector you
see inflation now coming

11:48.726 --> 11:51.686 align:middle
down because supply
chains have been fixed,

11:51.686 --> 11:54.536 align:middle
demand is shifting
back to services,

11:54.656 --> 11:57.836 align:middle
and shortages have been abated.

11:57.836 --> 12:00.166 align:middle
So you see that.

12:00.166 --> 12:04.296 align:middle
In the other-in the
housing services sector,

12:05.226 --> 12:09.976 align:middle
we expect inflation to continue
moving up for a while but then

12:10.226 --> 12:11.216 align:middle
to come down, assuming

12:11.216 --> 12:12.306 align:middle
that [rent increases associated
with] new leases continue

12:12.306 --> 12:12.746 align:middle
to be lower.

12:13.136 --> 12:15.246 align:middle
So, in those two sectors,
you've got a good story.

12:15.966 --> 12:19.766 align:middle
The issue is that we have a
large sector called nonhousing

12:19.766 --> 12:21.166 align:middle
service-core nonhousing
services,

12:21.166 --> 12:22.566 align:middle
where we don't see
disinflation yet.

12:23.276 --> 12:28.386 align:middle
But I would say that, so
far, what we see is progress

12:28.386 --> 12:31.156 align:middle
but without any weakening
in labor market conditions.

12:31.436 --> 12:32.536 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

12:32.536 --> 12:33.106 align:middle
Has your-I'm sorry.

12:33.156 --> 12:33.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:33.446 --> 12:34.056 align:middle
Go ahead. RACHEL SIEGEL.

12:34.056 --> 12:36.406 align:middle
Has your expectation for where
the unemployment rate might go

12:36.406 --> 12:37.416 align:middle
changed since December?

12:37.876 --> 12:38.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:38.366 --> 12:41.046 align:middle
No. We're going to
write down new forecasts

12:41.046 --> 12:43.056 align:middle
at the March meeting, and
we'll see at that time.

12:43.056 --> 12:44.916 align:middle
I will say that it is gratifying

12:44.916 --> 12:48.336 align:middle
to see the disinflationary
process now getting under way,

12:48.976 --> 12:51.386 align:middle
and we continue to get
strong labor market data.

12:51.386 --> 12:56.636 align:middle
So-but, you know, we'll update
those forecasts in March.

12:56.756 --> 12:58.446 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:58.566 --> 12:59.356 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

13:01.326 --> 13:02.076 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

13:02.076 --> 13:03.436 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

13:04.116 --> 13:06.386 align:middle
You and some of your colleagues
have emphasized the possibility

13:06.386 --> 13:08.996 align:middle
that job openings could come
down and that would let some

13:08.996 --> 13:11.396 align:middle
of the air out of the labor
market without major job losses.

13:11.716 --> 13:13.726 align:middle
We saw the opposite in the
December JOLTS this morning.

13:13.956 --> 13:16.206 align:middle
Job openings are
actually rising.

13:16.206 --> 13:19.796 align:middle
That also coincided with
slowdown in wage inflation.

13:20.266 --> 13:22.196 align:middle
Do you believe that openings
are an important indicator

13:22.196 --> 13:24.726 align:middle
to be studying to understand
where the labor market is

13:24.726 --> 13:26.086 align:middle
and where wage inflation
might be heading?

13:26.736 --> 13:27.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:27.026 --> 13:28.576 align:middle
So you're right about
the data, of course.

13:28.576 --> 13:32.026 align:middle
What we did see-we've seen
average hourly earnings

13:32.026 --> 13:36.406 align:middle
and now the employment cost
index abating a little bit still

13:36.746 --> 13:41.366 align:middle
off of their highs of six months
ago and more but still at levels

13:41.366 --> 13:43.506 align:middle
that are fairly elevated.

13:44.426 --> 13:46.956 align:middle
The job openings
number has-in JOLTS,

13:46.956 --> 13:49.456 align:middle
has been quite volatile
recently.

13:49.876 --> 13:51.866 align:middle
Yeah, I did see that it moved
up-back up this morning.

13:52.306 --> 13:56.106 align:middle
I do think that it's probably
an important indicator.

13:56.236 --> 13:59.846 align:middle
The ratio, I guess, is
back up to 1.9 job openings

13:59.846 --> 14:03.256 align:middle
to unemployed people, people
who are looking for work.

14:03.906 --> 14:07.136 align:middle
So it's an indicator, but
nonetheless, we-you're right,

14:07.136 --> 14:09.286 align:middle
we do see wages moving down.

14:09.286 --> 14:12.076 align:middle
If you look across the
rest of the labor market,

14:12.126 --> 14:15.836 align:middle
you still see very high
payroll job creation.

14:16.536 --> 14:20.686 align:middle
And, you know, quits are
still at an elevated level.

14:20.686 --> 14:22.726 align:middle
So many, many-by
many, many indicators,

14:23.316 --> 14:25.086 align:middle
the job market is
still very strong.

14:25.466 --> 14:28.446 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:28.446 --> 14:31.056 align:middle
Colby, and then Howard.

14:31.526 --> 14:32.346 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

14:32.346 --> 14:32.926 align:middle
Thank you.

14:32.976 --> 14:34.436 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the Financial Times.

14:34.816 --> 14:37.726 align:middle
Given the economic data
since the December meeting,

14:37.916 --> 14:39.696 align:middle
is the trajectory for
the fed funds rate

14:39.696 --> 14:42.896 align:middle
in the most recent SEP
still the best guidepost

14:42.896 --> 14:44.426 align:middle
for the policy path forward?

14:44.776 --> 14:48.286 align:middle
Or does ongoing now mean
more than two rate rises now?

14:49.926 --> 14:50.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:50.746 --> 14:51.376 align:middle
So you're right.

14:51.376 --> 14:54.366 align:middle
At the December meeting, we all
wrote down our best estimates

14:54.406 --> 14:56.606 align:middle
of what we thought the
ultimate level would be,

14:56.606 --> 14:58.616 align:middle
and that's obviously
back in December.

14:58.616 --> 15:01.616 align:middle
And the median for that was
between 5 and 5¼ percent.

15:02.436 --> 15:05.016 align:middle
At the March meeting,

15:05.016 --> 15:06.286 align:middle
we're going to update
those assessments.

15:06.286 --> 15:07.986 align:middle
We did not update them today.

15:08.276 --> 15:10.156 align:middle
We did, however, continue to say

15:10.156 --> 15:12.226 align:middle
that we believe ongoing rate
hikes will be appropriate

15:12.226 --> 15:15.566 align:middle
to attain a sufficiently
restrictive stance of policy

15:15.566 --> 15:18.306 align:middle
to bring inflation
back down to 2 percent.

15:18.886 --> 15:20.346 align:middle
We think we've covered
a lot of ground,

15:20.666 --> 15:24.286 align:middle
and financial conditions
have certainly tightened.

15:24.756 --> 15:27.686 align:middle
I would say we still think
there's work to do there.

15:27.996 --> 15:31.546 align:middle
We haven't made a decision on
exactly where that will be.

15:31.546 --> 15:33.536 align:middle
I think, you know, we're
going to be looking carefully

15:33.536 --> 15:35.776 align:middle
at the incoming data between
now and the March meeting

15:35.776 --> 15:38.556 align:middle
and then the May meeting.

15:38.556 --> 15:42.816 align:middle
I don't feel a lot of certainty
about where that will be.

15:42.816 --> 15:44.036 align:middle
It could certainly be higher

15:44.036 --> 15:45.146 align:middle
than we're writing
down right now.

15:45.556 --> 15:49.266 align:middle
If we come to the view that we
need to write down to, you know,

15:49.326 --> 15:51.846 align:middle
to move rates up beyond
what we said in December,

15:51.846 --> 15:52.956 align:middle
we would certainly do that.

15:52.956 --> 15:56.696 align:middle
At the same time, if the data
come in, in the other direction,

15:56.696 --> 15:58.916 align:middle
then we'll, you know, we'll
make data-dependent decisions

15:59.356 --> 16:00.546 align:middle
at coming meetings, of course.

16:00.786 --> 16:01.056 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

16:01.056 --> 16:03.276 align:middle
Just as a quick follow-up,
how are you viewing the kind

16:03.276 --> 16:06.526 align:middle
of balance of risk between
those two options of, you know,

16:06.566 --> 16:08.936 align:middle
the likelihood of maybe
falling short of that

16:08.936 --> 16:10.426 align:middle
or going beyond that level?

16:10.596 --> 16:11.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:11.936 --> 16:13.126 align:middle
I guess I would say it this way.

16:13.296 --> 16:18.956 align:middle
I continue to think
that it's very difficult

16:18.956 --> 16:23.216 align:middle
to manage the risk of doing
too little and finding out in 6

16:23.216 --> 16:26.036 align:middle
or 12 months that we
actually were close

16:26.036 --> 16:27.236 align:middle
but didn't get the job done.

16:27.236 --> 16:29.466 align:middle
Inflation springs back,
and we have to go back in.

16:29.766 --> 16:31.496 align:middle
And now, you really do worry

16:31.496 --> 16:33.646 align:middle
about expectations
getting unanchored

16:33.646 --> 16:34.586 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

16:34.896 --> 16:39.886 align:middle
This is a very difficult risk
to manage, whereas I-of course,

16:39.986 --> 16:43.016 align:middle
we have no incentive and
no desire to overtighten.

16:43.576 --> 16:46.406 align:middle
But we, you know, if we feel
like we've gone too far,

16:46.406 --> 16:49.496 align:middle
we can certainly-and
inflation is coming down faster

16:49.496 --> 16:52.696 align:middle
than we expect, then we have
tools that would work on that.

16:53.106 --> 16:55.736 align:middle
So I do think that,
in this situation,

16:55.736 --> 16:59.486 align:middle
where we have still the highest
inflation in 40 years, you know,

16:59.606 --> 17:01.226 align:middle
the job is not fully done.

17:01.526 --> 17:04.586 align:middle
As I started to mention
earlier, we have a sector

17:04.586 --> 17:07.646 align:middle
that represents 56 percent
of the core inflation index

17:07.646 --> 17:09.396 align:middle
where we don't see
disinflation yet.

17:09.966 --> 17:11.816 align:middle
So we don't see it.

17:11.816 --> 17:12.756 align:middle
It's not happening yet.

17:12.756 --> 17:16.116 align:middle
Inflation in the
core services ex.

17:16.696 --> 17:19.366 align:middle
housing is still
running at 4 percent

17:19.366 --> 17:20.836 align:middle
on a 6- and 12-month basis.

17:20.836 --> 17:22.436 align:middle
So there's not-nothing
happening there.

17:22.436 --> 17:24.586 align:middle
In the other two sectors
representing, you know,

17:24.716 --> 17:26.986 align:middle
less than 50 percent,
you actually, I think,

17:26.986 --> 17:29.086 align:middle
now have a story
that is credible,

17:29.436 --> 17:31.486 align:middle
that's coming together,
although you don't actually see

17:31.486 --> 17:33.566 align:middle
disinflation yet in
housing services,

17:33.566 --> 17:34.826 align:middle
but it's in the pipeline, right?

17:35.436 --> 17:38.406 align:middle
So, for the third sector,
we don't see anything here.

17:38.406 --> 17:41.196 align:middle
So I think it would be
premature-it would be very

17:41.196 --> 17:44.336 align:middle
premature to declare
victory or to think

17:44.336 --> 17:45.366 align:middle
that we've really got this.

17:45.366 --> 17:47.806 align:middle
We need to see-our
goal, of course,

17:47.806 --> 17:48.926 align:middle
is to bring inflation down.

17:49.046 --> 17:50.636 align:middle
And how do we get that done?

17:51.566 --> 17:53.706 align:middle
There are many, many
factors driving inflation

17:53.816 --> 17:57.136 align:middle
in that sector, and they
should be coming into play

17:57.136 --> 18:00.626 align:middle
to have inflation-the
disinflationary process begin

18:00.786 --> 18:01.346 align:middle
in that sector.

18:01.346 --> 18:02.646 align:middle
But, so far, we don't see that.

18:02.646 --> 18:05.016 align:middle
And I think until we
do, we see ourselves

18:05.016 --> 18:06.446 align:middle
as having a lot of
work left to do.

18:06.446 --> 18:06.906 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:07.466 --> 18:08.086 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

18:09.846 --> 18:12.176 align:middle
Hi, Howard Schneider with
Reuters, and thanks as usual.

18:12.236 --> 18:14.646 align:middle
So I just wanted to connect
a couple of dots here.

18:14.646 --> 18:17.976 align:middle
The statement made a
number of changes that seem

18:17.976 --> 18:19.336 align:middle
to be saying things
are getting better.

18:19.336 --> 18:21.406 align:middle
You're saying inflation
has eased That's new.

18:21.476 --> 18:25.516 align:middle
You've taken out
references to the war

18:25.516 --> 18:27.736 align:middle
in Ukraine that's
causing price increases.

18:27.736 --> 18:29.666 align:middle
You've taken out
references to the pandemic.

18:30.086 --> 18:31.986 align:middle
You've eliminated
all the reasons

18:31.986 --> 18:33.936 align:middle
that you said prices
were being driven higher,

18:34.296 --> 18:36.386 align:middle
yet that's not mapping
to any change

18:36.386 --> 18:37.976 align:middle
in how you describe policy.

18:38.036 --> 18:39.856 align:middle
We still have ongoing
increases to come.

18:40.546 --> 18:42.406 align:middle
So I'm wondering,
why is that the case?

18:42.856 --> 18:45.746 align:middle
And does it have more to do with
uncertainty around the outlook

18:46.156 --> 18:47.856 align:middle
or more to do with
you not wanting

18:47.856 --> 18:51.136 align:middle
to give a very overeager
market a reason to get ahead

18:51.136 --> 18:52.256 align:middle
of itself and overreact?

18:52.666 --> 18:54.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:54.416 --> 18:56.416 align:middle
So I guess I would
say it this way.

18:57.216 --> 18:59.766 align:middle
We can now say, I
think, for the first time

19:00.256 --> 19:02.776 align:middle
that the disinflationary
process has started.

19:03.266 --> 19:03.876 align:middle
We can see that.

19:03.876 --> 19:06.226 align:middle
And we see it, really,
in goods prices so far.

19:07.396 --> 19:08.806 align:middle
Goods prices is a big sector.

19:08.876 --> 19:11.156 align:middle
We-this is what we thought would
happen since the very beginning,

19:11.156 --> 19:12.936 align:middle
and now here it is
actually happening,

19:12.936 --> 19:14.076 align:middle
and for the reasons we thought.

19:14.076 --> 19:16.146 align:middle
We-you know, it's supply
chains, it's shortages,

19:16.416 --> 19:19.286 align:middle
and it's demand revolving
back towards services.

19:19.286 --> 19:20.506 align:middle
So this is a good thing.

19:20.646 --> 19:21.306 align:middle
This is a good thing.

19:21.796 --> 19:23.406 align:middle
But that's, you know,
around a quarter

19:23.406 --> 19:26.266 align:middle
of the PCE price
index-core PCE price index.

19:26.706 --> 19:31.486 align:middle
So the second sector is housing
services, and that's driven

19:31.486 --> 19:32.586 align:middle
by very different things.

19:32.666 --> 19:35.646 align:middle
And we-as I mentioned, with
housing services, we expect,

19:35.816 --> 19:37.226 align:middle
and other forecasters expect,

19:37.226 --> 19:38.896 align:middle
that measured inflation
will continue moving

19:38.896 --> 19:41.736 align:middle
up for several months
but will then come down,

19:42.506 --> 19:44.846 align:middle
assuming that [rent increases
associated with] new leases

19:45.066 --> 19:46.466 align:middle
continue to be soft.

19:47.036 --> 19:47.916 align:middle
And we do assume that.

19:47.916 --> 19:50.026 align:middle
So we think that that's
sort of in the pipeline.

19:50.026 --> 19:53.796 align:middle
And we actually see disinflation
in the goods sector,

19:53.796 --> 19:56.866 align:middle
and we see it in the pipeline
for two sectors that amount

19:56.866 --> 19:57.906 align:middle
to a little less than half.

19:57.906 --> 19:58.846 align:middle
So this is good.

19:58.846 --> 20:00.916 align:middle
And we note that when we
say inflation is coming

20:00.916 --> 20:01.836 align:middle
down that this is good.

20:02.806 --> 20:07.176 align:middle
We expect to see that that
disinflation process will be

20:07.176 --> 20:12.016 align:middle
seen, we hope soon,
in the core goods ex.

20:12.016 --> 20:14.076 align:middle
housing-sorry, the
core services ex.

20:14.076 --> 20:15.416 align:middle
housing sector that
I talked about.

20:16.016 --> 20:16.566 align:middle
We don't see it yet.

20:16.566 --> 20:19.416 align:middle
It's-you know, it's seven
or eight different kinds

20:19.416 --> 20:22.006 align:middle
of services, not all
of them are the same.

20:22.436 --> 20:24.396 align:middle
And, you know, we have a sense
of what's going on in each

20:24.396 --> 20:26.906 align:middle
of those different subsections.

20:26.906 --> 20:33.356 align:middle
Probably the biggest part of it,
probably 60 percent of that is,

20:33.726 --> 20:36.396 align:middle
you know, research would
show is sensitive to slack

20:36.396 --> 20:38.976 align:middle
in the economy and so the
labor market will probably

20:38.976 --> 20:39.496 align:middle
be important.

20:39.496 --> 20:41.526 align:middle
Some of the other ones it's-the
labor market is not going

20:41.526 --> 20:42.106 align:middle
to be important.

20:42.546 --> 20:43.896 align:middle
Many other factors
will drive it.

20:43.896 --> 20:47.056 align:middle
In any case, we don't see
disinflation in that sector yet.

20:47.316 --> 20:50.426 align:middle
And I think we need to
see that it's the majority

20:50.426 --> 20:52.316 align:middle
of the core PCE index,
which is the thing

20:52.316 --> 20:55.516 align:middle
that we think is the best
predictor of headline PCE,

20:55.516 --> 20:56.466 align:middle
which is [the price series that
we associate with] our mandate.

20:57.016 --> 20:59.906 align:middle
So it's not that we're
not-we're neither optimistic

20:59.906 --> 21:00.586 align:middle
nor pessimistic.

21:00.616 --> 21:04.116 align:middle
We're just telling you that we
don't see inflation moving down

21:04.116 --> 21:05.296 align:middle
yet in that large sector.

21:05.296 --> 21:08.436 align:middle
I think we will fairly soon,
but we don't see it yet.

21:08.436 --> 21:11.676 align:middle
Until we do, I think we-you
know, we see ourselves,

21:11.676 --> 21:13.806 align:middle
we've got to be honest with
ourselves, but we see ourselves

21:13.806 --> 21:16.706 align:middle
as having perhaps more
persistent-we'll see more

21:16.706 --> 21:18.266 align:middle
persistent inflation
in that sector,

21:18.766 --> 21:21.006 align:middle
which will take longer
to get down.

21:21.266 --> 21:24.186 align:middle
And we're just going to have
to-we have to complete the job.

21:24.186 --> 21:25.436 align:middle
You know, that's
what we're here for.

21:27.296 --> 21:28.396 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:29.056 --> 21:30.516 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

21:32.406 --> 21:33.736 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

21:34.076 --> 21:36.336 align:middle
Chair Powell, you
observed several years ago

21:36.336 --> 21:38.466 align:middle
that we learned we can have
a low unemployment rate

21:38.906 --> 21:40.396 align:middle
without above-target inflation.

21:40.396 --> 21:42.796 align:middle
And we have learned lately
that inflation can come

21:42.796 --> 21:46.436 align:middle
down from its uncomfortably high
level despite a historically low

21:46.436 --> 21:47.106 align:middle
unemployment rate.

21:47.516 --> 21:50.086 align:middle
Given that, and given how much
you did over the last year,

21:50.686 --> 21:52.946 align:middle
why do you think further
rate increases are needed?

21:53.136 --> 21:56.126 align:middle
Why not stop here and
see what transpires

21:56.126 --> 21:58.986 align:middle
in the coming months
before raising rates again?

22:00.026 --> 22:00.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:00.816 --> 22:03.266 align:middle
So we've-you know, we've raised
rates 4½ percentage points,

22:03.456 --> 22:07.346 align:middle
and we're talking about a
couple of more rate hikes to get

22:07.346 --> 22:09.626 align:middle
to that level we think is
appropriately restrictive.

22:10.256 --> 22:12.236 align:middle
And why do we think
that's probably necessary?

22:12.236 --> 22:14.786 align:middle
We think, because inflation
is still running very hot.

22:15.576 --> 22:18.466 align:middle
We're, of course, taking into
account long and variable lags,

22:18.516 --> 22:20.456 align:middle
and we're thinking about that.

22:20.586 --> 22:24.016 align:middle
It really-the story we're
telling about inflation is

22:24.296 --> 22:27.106 align:middle
to ourselves, and the way we
understand it is basically the

22:27.106 --> 22:29.256 align:middle
three things that I've just
gone through a couple of times.

22:29.646 --> 22:32.196 align:middle
And, again, we don't see
it affecting the services

22:32.196 --> 22:32.906 align:middle
sector ex.

22:33.536 --> 22:34.576 align:middle
housing yet.

22:35.066 --> 22:37.886 align:middle
But, I mean, I think
our assessment is

22:37.886 --> 22:39.756 align:middle
that we're not very
far from that level.

22:39.756 --> 22:42.216 align:middle
We don't know that, though.

22:42.216 --> 22:42.886 align:middle
We don't know that.

22:42.886 --> 22:45.106 align:middle
So I think we're-you know,
we're living in a world

22:45.106 --> 22:46.256 align:middle
of significant uncertainty.

22:46.686 --> 22:50.126 align:middle
I would look across the rate-the
spectrum of rates and see

22:50.126 --> 22:53.396 align:middle
that real rates are now
positive by-you know,

22:53.446 --> 22:55.986 align:middle
by an appropriate set
of measures are positive

22:55.986 --> 22:56.856 align:middle
across the yield curve.

22:56.856 --> 22:58.316 align:middle
I think policy is restrictive.

22:58.756 --> 23:00.566 align:middle
We're trying to make
a fine judgment

23:00.566 --> 23:02.686 align:middle
about how much is
restrictive enough.

23:02.686 --> 23:03.086 align:middle
That's all.

23:03.236 --> 23:04.926 align:middle
And we're going to-you know,
that's why we're slowing

23:04.926 --> 23:06.286 align:middle
down to 25 basis points.

23:06.576 --> 23:08.346 align:middle
We're going to be carefully
watching the economy

23:08.756 --> 23:10.896 align:middle
and watching inflation
and watching the progress

23:10.896 --> 23:12.216 align:middle
of the disinflationary process.

23:12.336 --> 23:12.776 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

23:13.076 --> 23:17.066 align:middle
Did you or your colleagues
discuss the conditions

23:17.066 --> 23:19.836 align:middle
for a pause at this
meeting this week?

23:20.106 --> 23:20.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:21.856 --> 23:23.806 align:middle
We-you know, you'll see
that the minutes will come

23:23.806 --> 23:25.616 align:middle
out in three weeks, and we'll
give you a lot of detail.

23:25.996 --> 23:27.956 align:middle
I-you know, we spend
a lot of time talking

23:27.956 --> 23:32.496 align:middle
about the path ahead and
the state of the economy.

23:32.876 --> 23:35.256 align:middle
And I wouldn't want to start

23:35.256 --> 23:37.356 align:middle
to drive the-describe
all the details there,

23:37.356 --> 23:38.676 align:middle
but that was the sense
of the discussion,

23:38.676 --> 23:41.836 align:middle
was really talking quite a
bit about the path forward.

23:43.696 --> 23:44.516 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:47.866 --> 23:48.286 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

23:48.286 --> 23:48.816 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

23:48.816 --> 23:51.136 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
the debt ceiling.

23:51.616 --> 23:54.626 align:middle
Given that we've now hit up
against it, I was wondering

23:54.626 --> 23:56.596 align:middle
if the U.S. goes
past the X date,

23:56.766 --> 23:59.726 align:middle
will the Fed do whatever the
Treasury directs as it relates

23:59.726 --> 24:01.516 align:middle
to making payments
as the fiscal agent

24:01.516 --> 24:04.676 align:middle
or will it do its own analysis
of any legal constraints?

24:04.886 --> 24:06.276 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:06.396 --> 24:09.056 align:middle
So your question is, would
we-say your question again.

24:09.056 --> 24:10.226 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

24:10.226 --> 24:13.286 align:middle
Will the Fed do what
Treasury directs as it relates

24:13.396 --> 24:16.716 align:middle
to making payments or will
it do its own analysis

24:16.716 --> 24:17.766 align:middle
of any legal constraints?

24:18.036 --> 24:18.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:18.316 --> 24:20.226 align:middle
So you're really asking
about-you're asking

24:20.556 --> 24:23.746 align:middle
about prioritization, in
effect, is what- VICTORIA GUIDA.

24:23.746 --> 24:24.426 align:middle
Yes. Yes. CHAIR POWELL.

24:24.426 --> 24:26.956 align:middle
Okay. So I feel like
I have to say this.

24:27.336 --> 24:30.056 align:middle
There's only one way forward
here, and that is for Congress

24:30.056 --> 24:31.096 align:middle
to raise the debt ceiling

24:31.096 --> 24:33.116 align:middle
so that the United States
government can pay all

24:33.116 --> 24:34.766 align:middle
of its obligations when due.

24:34.766 --> 24:38.426 align:middle
And any deviations from that
path would be highly risky

24:38.946 --> 24:40.606 align:middle
and that no one should assume

24:40.606 --> 24:43.576 align:middle
that the Fed can protect the
economy from the consequences

24:43.576 --> 24:45.276 align:middle
of failing to act
in a timely manner.

24:46.136 --> 24:47.756 align:middle
In terms of our relationship
with the Treasury,

24:47.756 --> 24:48.846 align:middle
we are their fiscal agent.

24:49.466 --> 24:50.636 align:middle
And I'm just going
to leave it at that.

24:50.916 --> 24:51.596 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

24:51.596 --> 24:54.186 align:middle
Are you actively doing any
planning of what might happen

24:54.186 --> 24:55.496 align:middle
in the event that
that would happen?

24:55.806 --> 24:56.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:56.356 --> 24:57.346 align:middle
I'm just going to
leave it at that.

24:57.346 --> 24:59.466 align:middle
This is a matter that's
to be resolved between,

24:59.876 --> 25:02.716 align:middle
really-it's really Congress'
job to raise the debt ceiling.

25:03.206 --> 25:05.526 align:middle
And I gather there are
discussions happening,

25:05.526 --> 25:06.526 align:middle
but they don't involve us.

25:06.526 --> 25:09.496 align:middle
We're not involved
in those discussions.

25:09.496 --> 25:11.686 align:middle
So we're the fiscal agent.

25:12.656 --> 25:13.816 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:14.006 --> 25:15.096 align:middle
Jeanna and then Steve.

25:18.586 --> 25:19.646 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

25:19.646 --> 25:20.906 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek from
the New York Times.

25:20.996 --> 25:22.236 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

25:22.936 --> 25:25.046 align:middle
I wonder, was there
any discussion today

25:25.046 --> 25:27.966 align:middle
of the possibility of
pausing rate increases

25:27.966 --> 25:29.206 align:middle
and then restarting them?

25:29.206 --> 25:31.466 align:middle
Lorie Logan from the Federal
Reserve Bank of Dallas seemed

25:31.466 --> 25:34.216 align:middle
to suggest that would be a
possibility in a recent speech.

25:34.216 --> 25:36.436 align:middle
And I wonder if that view is
broadly shared on the Committee.

25:37.946 --> 25:38.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:39.146 --> 25:42.996 align:middle
So the Committee
obviously did not see this

25:42.996 --> 25:43.766 align:middle
as the time to pause.

25:44.576 --> 25:46.456 align:middle
We judged that the
appropriate, you know,

25:46.616 --> 25:47.816 align:middle
thing to do at this meeting was

25:47.816 --> 25:50.346 align:middle
to raise the federal funds
rate by 25 basis points.

25:50.416 --> 25:52.306 align:middle
And we said that we
continue to anticipate

25:52.306 --> 25:55.306 align:middle
that ongoing increases in the
target range will be appropriate

25:55.376 --> 25:56.686 align:middle
in order to attain that stance

25:56.686 --> 25:58.936 align:middle
of sufficiently restrictive
monetary policy

25:58.936 --> 26:00.536 align:middle
that will bring inflation
down to 2 percent.

26:00.536 --> 26:02.736 align:middle
So that's the judgment
that we made.

26:02.736 --> 26:07.546 align:middle
You know, we're going to write
down new forecasts in March,

26:07.866 --> 26:11.676 align:middle
and we'll-you know, we'll
certainly be looking

26:11.676 --> 26:13.286 align:middle
at the incoming data
as everyone else will.

26:13.826 --> 26:14.186 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

26:14.186 --> 26:14.826 align:middle
Sorry, I should have
been clearer.

26:14.926 --> 26:17.356 align:middle
I mean, would it be possible
to take a meeting off,

26:17.426 --> 26:18.936 align:middle
for example, and then resume?

26:19.016 --> 26:22.036 align:middle
You know, could you, rather
than just doing at every meeting

26:22.036 --> 26:24.376 align:middle
that move, go a little
bit more slowly,

26:24.376 --> 26:28.356 align:middle
take some gaps in between moves?

26:28.356 --> 26:28.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:28.756 --> 26:30.506 align:middle
I mean, I think this
is not something

26:30.506 --> 26:32.216 align:middle
that the Committee is
thinking about or exploring

26:32.216 --> 26:33.256 align:middle
in any kind of detail.

26:33.666 --> 26:36.216 align:middle
In principle, though, you know,
we used to-the thing we used

26:36.216 --> 26:38.026 align:middle
to do was go every other
meeting, if you remember,

26:38.026 --> 26:40.446 align:middle
25 basis points, and that
was considered a fast pace.

26:41.816 --> 26:44.846 align:middle
So I think a lot of
options are available.

26:44.846 --> 26:47.306 align:middle
And I mean, you saw what
the Bank of Canada did and,

26:47.306 --> 26:48.926 align:middle
you know, they left it
that they're willing

26:48.926 --> 26:51.896 align:middle
to raise rates after pausing.

26:52.066 --> 26:53.766 align:middle
But this is not something

26:53.766 --> 26:58.206 align:middle
that the Federal Open Market
Committee is on the point

26:58.206 --> 26:59.556 align:middle
of deciding right now.

27:00.256 --> 27:01.176 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:02.056 --> 27:04.316 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

27:04.316 --> 27:05.056 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

27:05.056 --> 27:10.296 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, the SEP has
the PCE inflation rate

27:10.296 --> 27:12.336 align:middle
in 2023 at 3.1 percent.

27:12.866 --> 27:16.266 align:middle
Meanwhile, the three-month
annualized PCE is 2.1 percent,

27:16.816 --> 27:18.976 align:middle
and you've achieved
this without going

27:18.976 --> 27:21.366 align:middle
to your 5.1 percent funds rate,

27:21.366 --> 27:23.176 align:middle
which is what you have
penciled in for this year.

27:23.796 --> 27:25.176 align:middle
And you've also achieved it

27:25.176 --> 27:27.016 align:middle
without the 1 percentage
point increase

27:27.056 --> 27:28.306 align:middle
in the unemployment rate,

27:28.306 --> 27:30.046 align:middle
which you have penciled
in for this year.

27:30.366 --> 27:32.416 align:middle
I'm wondering if you've
considered the idea of whether

27:32.416 --> 27:36.876 align:middle
or not your understanding of the
inflation dynamic may be wrong,

27:36.906 --> 27:39.316 align:middle
and it's possible to
achieve these things

27:39.316 --> 27:42.156 align:middle
without raising rates
that high and also

27:42.156 --> 27:45.306 align:middle
without the surge
in unemployment.

27:45.306 --> 27:47.286 align:middle
And, specifically, I
wonder if you might comment

27:47.286 --> 27:51.386 align:middle
on the speech given by Vice
Chair Lael Brainard, who said,

27:51.386 --> 27:54.456 align:middle
"To the extent that inputs other
than wages may be responsible

27:54.456 --> 27:55.916 align:middle
in part for important
price increases

27:56.196 --> 27:59.106 align:middle
for some nonhousing services,
an unwinding of these factors."

27:59.106 --> 28:00.686 align:middle
In other words, it
may not be wages.

28:01.076 --> 28:04.156 align:middle
The idea that it may not
require unemployment rising

28:04.576 --> 28:06.726 align:middle
to get this sector of
inflation under control.

28:06.726 --> 28:07.426 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

28:08.776 --> 28:09.506 align:middle
So a couple of things.

28:09.506 --> 28:11.626 align:middle
First, on the forecast,

28:12.186 --> 28:17.126 align:middle
if-you're right-if you take very
short-term three-month, say,

28:17.126 --> 28:20.476 align:middle
measures of PCE-core
PCE inflation,

28:20.606 --> 28:21.666 align:middle
they're quite low right now.

28:21.666 --> 28:24.336 align:middle
But that's because that's
driven by, you know,

28:24.426 --> 28:28.066 align:middle
significantly negative
readings from goods inflation.

28:29.116 --> 28:31.826 align:middle
Most forecasters would think

28:31.826 --> 28:35.126 align:middle
that the significantly negative
readings will be transitory

28:36.146 --> 28:40.456 align:middle
and that goods inflation
will move up fairly soon,

28:40.456 --> 28:43.266 align:middle
back up to its longer-run
trend of something

28:43.266 --> 28:44.856 align:middle
around zero, something
like that.

28:44.856 --> 28:48.346 align:middle
So a lot of forecasts would
call for core PCE to go back

28:48.346 --> 28:50.496 align:middle
up to 4 percent by the middle
of the year, for example.

28:50.496 --> 28:53.046 align:middle
So that's really where
the sustainable level is.

28:53.436 --> 28:54.666 align:middle
It's more like at 4 percent.

28:54.666 --> 28:57.756 align:middle
So that would suggest
there's work left to do.

28:58.886 --> 29:02.466 align:middle
You know, let's say inflation
does come down much faster

29:02.466 --> 29:04.906 align:middle
than we expect, which
is possible.

29:05.116 --> 29:06.296 align:middle
As I mentioned, you know,

29:06.296 --> 29:08.086 align:middle
obviously our policy
is data dependent.

29:08.086 --> 29:08.996 align:middle
We would take that into account.

29:09.666 --> 29:14.856 align:middle
In terms of the non-sorry,
the core nonhousing services,

29:14.856 --> 29:16.406 align:middle
as I mentioned earlier,

29:16.996 --> 29:19.826 align:middle
it's a very diverse sector,
six or seven sectors.

29:19.826 --> 29:27.046 align:middle
And so sectors that represent 55
or 60 percent of that-subsectors

29:27.046 --> 29:32.816 align:middle
of that sector are-we think
are sensitive to slack

29:32.816 --> 29:35.436 align:middle
in the economy, sensitive to
the labor market in a way,

29:35.856 --> 29:37.456 align:middle
but some of the other
sectors are not.

29:37.686 --> 29:38.626 align:middle
And, for example, you know,

29:38.626 --> 29:41.676 align:middle
financial services is a big
sector that's really not driven

29:42.266 --> 29:46.406 align:middle
by labor markets-wages.

29:46.926 --> 29:49.956 align:middle
So that's why I said there
are a number of things

29:49.956 --> 29:52.006 align:middle
that will affect-take
restaurants, right?

29:52.006 --> 29:53.886 align:middle
So, clearly, labor is
important for restaurants

29:53.886 --> 29:55.066 align:middle
but so are food prices.

29:55.796 --> 29:58.176 align:middle
And, you know, transportation
services is going to be driven

29:58.176 --> 30:00.856 align:middle
by fuel prices, for example.

30:00.856 --> 30:03.356 align:middle
So there are lots of
things in that mix

30:03.896 --> 30:05.056 align:middle
that will drive inflation.

30:05.396 --> 30:08.076 align:middle
I would say overall,
though, my own view would be

30:08.446 --> 30:11.776 align:middle
that you're not going to have,
you know, a sustainable return

30:11.776 --> 30:13.746 align:middle
to 2 percent inflation
in that sector

30:14.326 --> 30:16.366 align:middle
without a better balance
in the labor market.

30:16.686 --> 30:20.296 align:middle
And I don't know what
that will require in terms

30:20.296 --> 30:22.316 align:middle
of increased unemployment,
your question.

30:22.866 --> 30:25.606 align:middle
I do think there are
a number of dimensions

30:25.606 --> 30:27.286 align:middle
through which the labor
market can soften.

30:27.886 --> 30:31.416 align:middle
And so far, we've got-as
I mentioned, in goods,

30:31.416 --> 30:32.546 align:middle
we have inflation moving

30:32.546 --> 30:34.546 align:middle
down without the softening
in the labor market.

30:34.996 --> 30:36.666 align:middle
I think most forecasters
would say

30:36.666 --> 30:40.486 align:middle
that unemployment will
probably rise a bit from here.

30:40.486 --> 30:43.526 align:middle
But I still think-I continue
to think that there's a path

30:43.646 --> 30:46.326 align:middle
to getting inflation
back down to 2 percent

30:46.326 --> 30:49.466 align:middle
without a really
significant economic decline

30:49.466 --> 30:51.906 align:middle
or a significant
increase in unemployment.

30:52.426 --> 30:55.906 align:middle
And that's because the-you
know, the setting we're

30:55.906 --> 30:57.196 align:middle
in is quite different.

30:57.316 --> 31:01.326 align:middle
The inflation that we originally
got was very much a collision

31:01.486 --> 31:05.766 align:middle
between very strong demand
and hard supply constraints,

31:05.766 --> 31:09.596 align:middle
not something that you really
have seen in prior-you know,

31:09.596 --> 31:10.816 align:middle
in prior business cycles.

31:10.886 --> 31:12.906 align:middle
And so now we see
goods inflation coming

31:12.906 --> 31:14.826 align:middle
down for the reasons we thought,

31:15.066 --> 31:18.566 align:middle
and we understand why housing
inflation will come down.

31:18.566 --> 31:20.226 align:middle
And I think will-a
story will emerge

31:20.226 --> 31:24.386 align:middle
on the nonhousing services
sector soon enough.

31:24.486 --> 31:26.856 align:middle
But I think there is-there's
ongoing disinflation,

31:26.856 --> 31:29.536 align:middle
and we don't yet see
weakening in the labor market.

31:29.536 --> 31:30.806 align:middle
So we'll have to see.

31:30.806 --> 31:31.876 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

31:32.146 --> 31:33.866 align:middle
Can we get there with 5 percent?

31:33.866 --> 31:34.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:34.976 --> 31:35.766 align:middle
Certainly possible.

31:35.996 --> 31:37.286 align:middle
Yeah. Absolutely it's possible.

31:37.796 --> 31:40.046 align:middle
You know, it's a
question-no one really knows.

31:40.046 --> 31:42.276 align:middle
I think it's because this is not

31:42.276 --> 31:44.616 align:middle
like the other business
cycles in so many ways.

31:45.286 --> 31:49.736 align:middle
It may well be that as-yeah,

31:49.736 --> 31:52.386 align:middle
as-that it will take
more slowing

31:52.456 --> 31:55.876 align:middle
than we expect-than I expect to
get inflation down to 2 percent.

31:56.096 --> 31:58.296 align:middle
But I don't-that's
not my base case.

31:58.296 --> 32:03.926 align:middle
My base case is that
the economy can return

32:03.926 --> 32:07.046 align:middle
to 2 percent inflation without
a really significant downturn

32:07.046 --> 32:09.046 align:middle
or a really big increase
in unemployment.

32:09.046 --> 32:10.816 align:middle
I think that's a
possible outcome.

32:11.596 --> 32:15.446 align:middle
I think many, many forecasters
would say it's not the most

32:15.446 --> 32:16.126 align:middle
likely outcome.

32:16.126 --> 32:18.556 align:middle
But I would say there's
a chance of it.

32:18.556 --> 32:19.106 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

32:21.286 --> 32:23.016 align:middle
Michael. MICHAEL MCKEE.

32:24.566 --> 32:26.586 align:middle
Michael McKee from
Bloomberg TV and Radio.

32:26.586 --> 32:28.896 align:middle
I'd like to pick up on
what you were just saying

32:28.896 --> 32:33.636 align:middle
about a substantial downturn
and ask, with the full weight

32:33.746 --> 32:39.286 align:middle
of your tightening not in
place yet and with the progress

32:39.286 --> 32:42.866 align:middle
against inflation, there's
still a lot of talk about very,

32:42.866 --> 32:46.846 align:middle
very slow growth
going forward in 2023.

32:47.306 --> 32:51.966 align:middle
And the recession indicators are
all suggesting that we are going

32:51.966 --> 32:53.126 align:middle
to see recession this year.

32:53.126 --> 32:55.416 align:middle
So I'm wondering if
you've changed your view

32:55.416 --> 32:59.226 align:middle
or you have a more nuanced view
of what you think the danger

32:59.226 --> 33:02.426 align:middle
to economic growth
is going forward

33:02.696 --> 33:06.766 align:middle
and whether you're very
close to perhaps tipping it

33:06.766 --> 33:08.196 align:middle
into the wrong place,

33:08.196 --> 33:09.886 align:middle
which calls for more
restraint on your part.

33:10.056 --> 33:10.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:11.106 --> 33:14.196 align:middle
So I do think you-most
forecasts and, you know,

33:14.196 --> 33:15.806 align:middle
my own assessment would be

33:15.806 --> 33:18.966 align:middle
that that growth will
continue-positive growth will

33:18.966 --> 33:21.966 align:middle
continue but at a subdued
pace as it did last year.

33:21.966 --> 33:25.656 align:middle
We had growth of-GDP growth
of 1 percent last year

33:25.656 --> 33:28.086 align:middle
and also final sales growth,

33:28.156 --> 33:30.646 align:middle
which we think is a better
indicator, of about 1 percent.

33:31.086 --> 33:32.776 align:middle
I think, you know,
most forecasts

33:32.826 --> 33:34.846 align:middle
and certainly my
assessment would be

33:34.846 --> 33:39.846 align:middle
that growth will continue at a
fairly subdued level this year.

33:41.106 --> 33:43.676 align:middle
There are other factors, though,
that need to be considered.

33:43.676 --> 33:50.446 align:middle
You will have seen that the
global picture is improving a

33:50.506 --> 33:52.506 align:middle
bit, and that will matter
for us, potentially.

33:52.876 --> 33:55.216 align:middle
The labor market remains
very, very strong,

33:55.906 --> 33:58.996 align:middle
and that's job creation,
that's wages.

33:59.146 --> 34:03.326 align:middle
As inflation does come down,
sentiment will improve.

34:04.196 --> 34:08.816 align:middle
You also-state and local
governments are really flush

34:08.816 --> 34:11.976 align:middle
these days with,
you know, money,

34:11.976 --> 34:14.836 align:middle
and many of them are considering
tax cuts or even sending checks.

34:15.376 --> 34:16.546 align:middle
So I think that's going

34:16.546 --> 34:18.176 align:middle
to support-they're
also spending a lot.

34:18.176 --> 34:20.736 align:middle
There's a lot of spending coming
in the construction pipeline,

34:21.376 --> 34:22.716 align:middle
both private and public.

34:22.926 --> 34:24.806 align:middle
And so that's going to
support economic activity.

34:24.806 --> 34:27.806 align:middle
So I think there's a good chance

34:27.846 --> 34:31.016 align:middle
that those factors will
help support positive growth

34:31.056 --> 34:31.406 align:middle
this year.

34:32.066 --> 34:33.656 align:middle
And that's my base case, is

34:33.656 --> 34:35.796 align:middle
that there will be
positive growth this year.

34:36.256 --> 34:37.136 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

34:37.466 --> 34:38.726 align:middle
Okay, Rich.

34:40.626 --> 34:41.526 align:middle
RICH MILLER.

34:41.696 --> 34:41.966 align:middle
Thank you.

34:42.316 --> 34:43.266 align:middle
Rich Miller from Bloomberg.

34:43.266 --> 34:44.756 align:middle
First off, how are you doing?

34:44.756 --> 34:45.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:45.606 --> 34:46.096 align:middle
Fine. Thanks.

34:46.286 --> 34:47.016 align:middle
Fine. RICH MILLER.

34:47.326 --> 34:50.066 align:middle
Good. Second off, I think,

34:50.206 --> 34:53.096 align:middle
earlier on in the press
conference, you said you need

34:53.096 --> 34:54.856 align:middle
to see substantially
more evidence

34:54.856 --> 34:57.816 align:middle
of inflation coming down.

34:57.866 --> 35:00.206 align:middle
Can you give us some idea
of what you're thinking of?

35:00.206 --> 35:02.286 align:middle
You mentioned three months-that
we've seen three months

35:02.286 --> 35:02.656 align:middle
in a row.

35:02.726 --> 35:05.716 align:middle
Governor Waller suggested he
might want to see six months.

35:05.716 --> 35:08.166 align:middle
And so is it just the
inflation data, or do you have

35:08.166 --> 35:12.436 align:middle
to see the labor market coming
back into better balance to have

35:12.436 --> 35:14.246 align:middle
that "substantially
more evidence" metric?

35:14.906 --> 35:15.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:15.266 --> 35:16.656 align:middle
So I don't think
there's, you know,

35:16.656 --> 35:18.796 align:middle
going to be a light switch
flipped or anything like that.

35:18.796 --> 35:21.186 align:middle
I think it's just an
accumulating-accumulation

35:21.186 --> 35:21.766 align:middle
of evidence.

35:22.066 --> 35:24.036 align:middle
So, of course, we'll
be looking-by the time

35:24.236 --> 35:27.096 align:middle
of the March meeting, we'll have
two more employment reports,

35:27.096 --> 35:29.806 align:middle
two more CPI reports,
and we'll be looking

35:29.806 --> 35:31.846 align:middle
at those carefully
as all of us will.

35:32.306 --> 35:35.776 align:middle
And we'll be asking ourselves,
what are they telling us?

35:36.446 --> 35:41.316 align:middle
And soon after that, we'll have
another ECI wage report, which,

35:41.316 --> 35:45.486 align:middle
as you know, is a report that
we like because it adjusts

35:45.486 --> 35:47.436 align:middle
for composition and
it's very complete.

35:48.096 --> 35:50.486 align:middle
And, you know, the one we
got, I guess it was yesterday,

35:50.486 --> 35:52.366 align:middle
was some-was constructive.

35:52.366 --> 35:55.086 align:middle
It's-you know, it
shows wages coming down

35:55.086 --> 35:56.666 align:middle
but still at a high level.

35:56.666 --> 36:00.056 align:middle
They're still at a level
that's way above-well

36:00.056 --> 36:03.696 align:middle
above where they were
before the pandemic.

36:03.966 --> 36:07.386 align:middle
So I don't want to put a number
on it in terms of months,

36:07.436 --> 36:10.766 align:middle
but as the accumulated
evidence comes in,

36:11.126 --> 36:13.656 align:middle
it's going to be reflected in
our assessment of the outlook,

36:14.046 --> 36:16.696 align:middle
and that will be reflected
in our policy over time.

36:17.176 --> 36:21.146 align:middle
But I will say, though,
we-you know, it is our job

36:21.146 --> 36:24.446 align:middle
to restore price stability and
achieve 2 percent inflation

36:24.446 --> 36:25.766 align:middle
for the benefit of
the American public.

36:25.766 --> 36:28.636 align:middle
We're not-market participants
have a very different job.

36:28.956 --> 36:29.696 align:middle
It's a fine job.

36:29.696 --> 36:30.396 align:middle
It's a great job.

36:30.526 --> 36:32.776 align:middle
In fact, I did that
job for years

36:33.286 --> 36:35.926 align:middle
but in one form or another.

36:36.426 --> 36:37.896 align:middle
But, you know, we
have to deliver that.

36:38.156 --> 36:43.466 align:middle
And so we are strongly
resolved that we will, you know,

36:43.466 --> 36:46.506 align:middle
complete this task because we
think it has benefits that will,

36:47.286 --> 36:49.756 align:middle
you know, support
economic activity

36:49.756 --> 36:52.126 align:middle
and benefit the public
for many, many years.

36:54.676 --> 36:54.976 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:54.976 --> 36:56.936 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

36:57.326 --> 36:57.896 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

36:57.896 --> 37:00.036 align:middle
Thank you, Fed Chairman,
for taking the questions.

37:00.406 --> 37:03.896 align:middle
So you've talked about
we had solid job growth.

37:03.896 --> 37:05.356 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from Fox
Business, by the way.

37:05.356 --> 37:07.706 align:middle
We had solid job
growth, a slight falling

37:07.706 --> 37:09.226 align:middle
in the increase in
consumer spending.

37:09.846 --> 37:13.046 align:middle
It seems so far it's been
relatively mild from the economy

37:13.046 --> 37:15.716 align:middle
to go to-from a 9.1
percent CPI inflation

37:15.716 --> 37:17.476 align:middle
to 6.5 percent CPI inflation.

37:18.006 --> 37:20.666 align:middle
Is the hard part yet to
come to go from 6.5 to 2?

37:21.326 --> 37:21.846 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:21.846 --> 37:23.386 align:middle
I don't think we know, honestly.

37:23.996 --> 37:29.636 align:middle
You know, the-so we, of
course, expected goods inflation

37:29.636 --> 37:33.426 align:middle
to start coming down
by the end of 2021,

37:34.266 --> 37:36.626 align:middle
and it didn't come
down all through '22.

37:36.626 --> 37:38.586 align:middle
And now it's coming down, and
it's come down pretty fast.

37:39.006 --> 37:42.406 align:middle
So I would say these are-this
is not a standard business cycle

37:42.406 --> 37:45.796 align:middle
where you can look at the last
10 times there was a global

37:45.796 --> 37:47.776 align:middle
pandemic and we shut
the economy down

37:48.616 --> 37:51.396 align:middle
and Congress did what it
did and we did what we did.

37:51.396 --> 37:52.546 align:middle
It's just-it's unique.

37:52.546 --> 37:56.116 align:middle
So I think certainty is
just not appropriate here.

37:56.356 --> 37:58.716 align:middle
Inflation-it's just harder
to forecast inflation.

37:59.036 --> 38:00.186 align:middle
It may come down faster.

38:00.266 --> 38:01.936 align:middle
It may take longer to come down.

38:02.456 --> 38:05.036 align:middle
And, you know, our job is
to deliver inflation back

38:05.036 --> 38:06.256 align:middle
to target, and we will do that.

38:06.256 --> 38:08.516 align:middle
But I think we're
going to be cautious

38:08.576 --> 38:12.576 align:middle
about declaring victory and,
you know, sending signals

38:12.576 --> 38:15.976 align:middle
that we think that the game
is won because, you know,

38:15.976 --> 38:17.076 align:middle
it's-we've got a long way to go.

38:17.076 --> 38:19.506 align:middle
It's just-it's the early
stages of disinflation.

38:19.506 --> 38:22.736 align:middle
And it's most welcome to be
able to say that we are now

38:23.236 --> 38:26.386 align:middle
in disinflation,
but that's great.

38:26.496 --> 38:29.336 align:middle
But we just see that it has
to spread through the economy

38:29.336 --> 38:30.406 align:middle
and that it's going
to take some time.

38:30.486 --> 38:30.856 align:middle
That's all.

38:31.036 --> 38:31.396 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

38:31.396 --> 38:33.686 align:middle
Do you-how long do you see then,
the federal funds rate remaining

38:33.686 --> 38:34.516 align:middle
at this elevated level?

38:35.116 --> 38:35.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:35.776 --> 38:38.606 align:middle
You know, so-again, my forecast
and that of my colleagues

38:38.606 --> 38:40.536 align:middle
as you will see from
the SEP and-I mean,

38:40.536 --> 38:43.146 align:middle
there are many different
forecasts, but, generally,

38:43.486 --> 38:46.476 align:middle
it's a forecast of slower
growth, some softening

38:46.476 --> 38:50.066 align:middle
in labor market conditions,
and inflation moving

38:50.066 --> 38:51.866 align:middle
down steadily but not quickly.

38:52.416 --> 38:57.276 align:middle
And, in that case, if the
economy performs broadly in line

38:57.276 --> 38:59.416 align:middle
with those expectations,
it will not be appropriate

38:59.416 --> 39:02.726 align:middle
to cut rates this year, to
loosen policy this year.

39:03.226 --> 39:06.176 align:middle
Of course, other people have
forecasts with inflation coming

39:06.176 --> 39:07.726 align:middle
down much faster,
that's a different thing.

39:07.726 --> 39:09.566 align:middle
You know, if that
happens-inflation comes

39:09.566 --> 39:12.006 align:middle
down much faster, you know,
then we'll be seeing that,

39:12.006 --> 39:14.406 align:middle
and it will be incorporated
into our thinking about policy.

39:14.406 --> 39:15.566 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:16.236 --> 39:19.666 align:middle
Simon. SIMON RABINOVITCH.

39:20.226 --> 39:21.016 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

39:21.016 --> 39:22.656 align:middle
Simon Rabinovitch
with the Economist.

39:23.056 --> 39:25.156 align:middle
May I ask a further
question about the language

39:25.156 --> 39:27.056 align:middle
around "ongoing increases?"

39:27.406 --> 39:30.646 align:middle
That, of course, implies at
least two further rate rises.

39:30.786 --> 39:33.396 align:middle
If you look at fed fund
and futures pricing,

39:33.736 --> 39:36.506 align:middle
the implication is that you'll
raise rates one more time

39:36.606 --> 39:37.786 align:middle
and then pause.

39:38.156 --> 39:41.776 align:middle
Are you concerned about that
divergence or do you think,

39:41.776 --> 39:43.976 align:middle
if everything breaks right,
is that a plausible outcome?

39:44.626 --> 39:46.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:46.446 --> 39:49.666 align:middle
I'm not particularly concerned
about the divergence, no,

39:49.666 --> 39:52.616 align:middle
because it is largely due
to the market's expectation

39:52.616 --> 39:54.366 align:middle
that inflation will
move down more quickly.

39:54.486 --> 39:58.656 align:middle
I think that's the
bigger part of that.

39:59.106 --> 40:02.166 align:middle
So, again, as I just
mentioned, we-you know,

40:02.166 --> 40:04.826 align:middle
our forecasts-different
participants have different

40:04.826 --> 40:06.836 align:middle
forecasts, but, generally,
those forecasts are

40:06.836 --> 40:10.676 align:middle
for continued subdued
growth, some softening

40:10.676 --> 40:13.626 align:middle
in the labor market but not
a recession, not a recession.

40:14.166 --> 40:18.096 align:middle
And we have inflation
moving down, you know,

40:18.096 --> 40:19.966 align:middle
into the-somewhere
in the mid-threes

40:19.966 --> 40:21.246 align:middle
or maybe lower than
that this year.

40:21.246 --> 40:22.386 align:middle
We'll update that in March,

40:22.386 --> 40:23.516 align:middle
but that's what we
thought in December.

40:24.156 --> 40:25.766 align:middle
Markets are past that.

40:25.806 --> 40:27.726 align:middle
They show inflation
coming down, in some cases,

40:27.726 --> 40:28.606 align:middle
much quicker than that.

40:29.056 --> 40:30.246 align:middle
So we'll just have to see.

40:30.506 --> 40:33.526 align:middle
And we have a different
view and-a different view,

40:33.526 --> 40:34.786 align:middle
it's a different
forecast, really.

40:35.336 --> 40:40.266 align:middle
And given our outlook, I just-I
don't see us cutting rates this

40:40.266 --> 40:42.486 align:middle
year if we get-if our
outlook turns true,

40:42.886 --> 40:43.886 align:middle
as I mentioned just now.

40:44.196 --> 40:46.956 align:middle
If we do see inflation coming
down much more quickly,

40:47.396 --> 40:49.136 align:middle
that'll play into our
policy setting, of course.

40:49.736 --> 40:50.016 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:54.116 --> 40:54.666 align:middle
Scott. SCOTT HORSLEY.

40:54.936 --> 40:55.626 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

40:55.626 --> 40:56.396 align:middle
Scott Horsley from NPR.

40:56.396 --> 41:00.186 align:middle
One of the changes in the
statement this month is

41:00.186 --> 41:02.036 align:middle
that the Committee is no
longer listing public health

41:02.036 --> 41:04.326 align:middle
as among the data
points you'll consider

41:04.326 --> 41:05.586 align:middle
in assessing conditions.

41:06.916 --> 41:08.256 align:middle
What should we make of that?

41:08.256 --> 41:10.956 align:middle
Does the Federal Reserve
no longer see the pandemic

41:10.956 --> 41:12.496 align:middle
as weighing on the economy?

41:13.066 --> 41:14.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:14.856 --> 41:16.306 align:middle
That's the general sense of it.

41:16.306 --> 41:18.756 align:middle
Look, we understand-I
personally understand well

41:18.756 --> 41:20.556 align:middle
that COVID is still out there,

41:21.346 --> 41:27.486 align:middle
but that it's no longer
playing an important role

41:27.486 --> 41:28.436 align:middle
in our economy.

41:28.436 --> 41:30.196 align:middle
And, you know, we've kept
that statement in there

41:30.196 --> 41:33.826 align:middle
for quite a while, and I think
we just-we knew we would take it

41:33.826 --> 41:34.626 align:middle
out at some point.

41:34.626 --> 41:38.076 align:middle
There's never a perfect time,
but we thought that-you know,

41:38.076 --> 41:39.886 align:middle
people are handling it
better, and the economy

41:39.886 --> 41:41.566 align:middle
and the society are
handling it better now.

41:41.566 --> 41:43.816 align:middle
It doesn't really need
to be in a-you know,

41:43.816 --> 41:48.616 align:middle
in the Fed's monthly, you
know, postmeeting statement

41:48.616 --> 41:51.086 align:middle
as an ongoing economic
risk as opposed to,

41:51.396 --> 41:52.636 align:middle
you know, a health issue.

41:54.526 --> 41:54.876 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:54.876 --> 41:58.406 align:middle
Nancy. NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

41:58.786 --> 41:59.476 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

41:59.506 --> 42:02.186 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

42:02.646 --> 42:04.486 align:middle
I wanted to go back
to another thing

42:04.486 --> 42:07.046 align:middle
that Fed Vice Chair Lael
Brainard said recently.

42:08.036 --> 42:12.456 align:middle
She said she doesn't see
signs of a wage-price spiral,

42:13.026 --> 42:15.386 align:middle
and I'm wondering if
you agree with that.

42:15.386 --> 42:16.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:16.326 --> 42:17.886 align:middle
I do. Yeah, I do.

42:17.916 --> 42:19.286 align:middle
You don't see that yet.

42:19.286 --> 42:21.806 align:middle
But the whole point is, you
know, if you-once you see it,

42:22.486 --> 42:24.376 align:middle
you have a serious problem.

42:24.556 --> 42:28.246 align:middle
That means that, effectively,
in people's decisionmaking,

42:28.246 --> 42:31.006 align:middle
inflation has become a
really salient issue.

42:31.006 --> 42:34.826 align:middle
And once that happens-that's
what we can't allow to happen.

42:35.066 --> 42:38.316 align:middle
And, you know, so that's why
we worry that the longer we're

42:38.316 --> 42:39.896 align:middle
at this and the longer
people are talking

42:39.896 --> 42:42.706 align:middle
about inflation all day
long, every day, you know,

42:43.126 --> 42:44.816 align:middle
the more risk of
something like that.

42:44.816 --> 42:47.526 align:middle
But no, there's not
much-it's more of a risk.

42:47.526 --> 42:50.516 align:middle
It always has been more of
a risk than anything else.

42:50.916 --> 42:52.716 align:middle
By the way, I think it's
becoming less salient.

42:53.136 --> 42:56.056 align:middle
And people are-you know, we
pick that up in conversations.

42:56.056 --> 42:58.396 align:middle
And I've seen some data, too,
that show people are, you know,

42:58.426 --> 43:01.426 align:middle
gradually-they're glad that
inflation is coming down.

43:01.426 --> 43:03.116 align:middle
People really don't
like inflation.

43:03.496 --> 43:04.996 align:middle
And as we see it coming down,

43:05.416 --> 43:07.626 align:middle
that could also add a
boost to economic activity.

43:07.936 --> 43:10.696 align:middle
You look at the sentiment
surveys now, and they're very,

43:10.696 --> 43:14.736 align:middle
very low with 3½ percent
unemployment and, you know,

43:14.736 --> 43:17.836 align:middle
high wage increases nominally
by historical standards.

43:18.036 --> 43:18.766 align:middle
Why can that be?

43:18.766 --> 43:20.006 align:middle
It has to be inflation, right?

43:20.446 --> 43:23.196 align:middle
So I think once inflation
is seen to be coming

43:23.196 --> 43:26.706 align:middle
down in coming months, even
you will also see a boost

43:26.706 --> 43:27.446 align:middle
to sentiment, I hope.

43:27.476 --> 43:28.196 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

43:28.196 --> 43:29.776 align:middle
So that's what you're
looking at most closely,

43:29.776 --> 43:31.276 align:middle
is consumer expectations?

43:32.066 --> 43:32.346 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:32.436 --> 43:35.096 align:middle
That's at the very heart, is
consumers and businesses that,

43:35.096 --> 43:40.236 align:middle
you know, are the-essentially,
we believe that expectations

43:40.346 --> 43:43.646 align:middle
of future inflation are a very
important part of the process

43:44.356 --> 43:45.476 align:middle
of creating inflation.

43:45.646 --> 43:48.396 align:middle
That's a sort of bedrock belief.

43:48.396 --> 43:52.026 align:middle
In one way or another,
it has to be.

43:52.026 --> 43:52.936 align:middle
We think it's important.

43:53.506 --> 44:00.026 align:middle
And, in this case, I would say,
the risk eight months ago or so,

44:00.616 --> 44:03.106 align:middle
longer-term inflation
expectations had moved up.

44:03.106 --> 44:04.916 align:middle
We moved quite vigorously
last year.

44:05.176 --> 44:07.946 align:middle
Expectations are-seem
to be well anchored,

44:07.946 --> 44:10.786 align:middle
including at the shorter end
now, not just the longer end.

44:10.786 --> 44:14.436 align:middle
So it's, you know-and that's-I
think that's very reassuring.

44:14.526 --> 44:16.436 align:middle
I think, you know, the
markets have decided,

44:16.436 --> 44:19.546 align:middle
and the public has decided, that
inflation is going to come back

44:19.546 --> 44:21.056 align:middle
down to 2 percent and
it's just a matter

44:21.056 --> 44:22.126 align:middle
of us following through.

44:22.436 --> 44:25.036 align:middle
That's immeasurably
helpful to the process

44:25.036 --> 44:26.026 align:middle
of getting inflation down.

44:26.336 --> 44:29.336 align:middle
The fact that people
now do generally believe

44:29.336 --> 44:30.906 align:middle
that it will come
down, that'll be part

44:30.906 --> 44:32.046 align:middle
of the process of
getting it down.

44:32.046 --> 44:33.306 align:middle
And it's a very positive thing.

44:35.166 --> 44:36.426 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:36.426 --> 44:38.766 align:middle
Greg. GREG ROBB.

44:39.416 --> 44:40.266 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

44:40.266 --> 44:42.026 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

44:42.486 --> 44:46.936 align:middle
In the minutes of the December
meeting, there was a couple

44:46.936 --> 44:49.726 align:middle
of sentences that struck
people as important,

44:49.726 --> 44:52.156 align:middle
when the Committee said
participants talked

44:52.156 --> 44:55.296 align:middle
about this unwarranted easing of
financial conditions was a risk

44:55.956 --> 45:00.396 align:middle
and it would make your life
harder to bring inflation down.

45:00.396 --> 45:02.946 align:middle
I haven't seen-heard
you talk much

45:02.946 --> 45:04.496 align:middle
about that today or
in the statement.

45:04.496 --> 45:07.406 align:middle
So I was wondering, has that
concern eased among members,

45:07.406 --> 45:09.576 align:middle
or is that still something
you're concerned about?

45:09.986 --> 45:10.236 align:middle
Thank you.

45:10.656 --> 45:10.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:10.936 --> 45:11.736 align:middle
I would put it this way.

45:11.736 --> 45:13.596 align:middle
It's something that
we monitor carefully.

45:13.696 --> 45:15.536 align:middle
Financial conditions
didn't really change much

45:15.536 --> 45:17.436 align:middle
from the December
meeting to now.

45:18.066 --> 45:20.486 align:middle
They mostly went sideways
or up and down but came

45:20.486 --> 45:22.866 align:middle
out in roughly the same place.

45:22.866 --> 45:25.586 align:middle
It's important that the markets
do reflect the tightening

45:25.586 --> 45:26.496 align:middle
that we're putting in place.

45:26.696 --> 45:30.216 align:middle
As we've discussed a couple
times here, there's a difference

45:30.216 --> 45:32.546 align:middle
in perspective by
some market measures

45:32.546 --> 45:34.156 align:middle
on how fast inflation
will come down.

45:34.876 --> 45:35.906 align:middle
We're just going to have to see.

45:36.286 --> 45:38.606 align:middle
I mean, I'm not going to
try to persuade people

45:38.606 --> 45:41.556 align:middle
to have a different
forecast, but our forecast is

45:41.556 --> 45:43.416 align:middle
that it will take some
time and some patience

45:43.416 --> 45:45.906 align:middle
and that we'll need to keep
rates higher for longer.

45:45.906 --> 45:46.606 align:middle
But we'll see.

45:46.606 --> 45:48.376 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:48.376 --> 45:49.436 align:middle
Brendan, for the last question.

45:50.226 --> 45:51.546 align:middle
BRENDAN PEDERSEN.

45:51.736 --> 45:52.596 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

45:52.806 --> 45:54.596 align:middle
Brendon Pedersen
with Punchbowl News.

45:54.696 --> 45:58.506 align:middle
I wanted to ask if the
Fed takes into account

45:58.506 --> 46:00.946 align:middle
at all the debt ceiling
when it comes

46:00.946 --> 46:04.846 align:middle
to quantitative tightening,
given the fact that rapid

46:04.846 --> 46:07.736 align:middle
or faster quantitative
tightening could bring us

46:07.766 --> 46:11.696 align:middle
closer, faster to that
drop-dead debt ceiling deadline.

46:11.756 --> 46:14.016 align:middle
Could it play in
effect as we get closer

46:14.266 --> 46:16.596 align:middle
to that drop-dead
deadline this summer?

46:19.286 --> 46:20.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:20.306 --> 46:22.906 align:middle
Look, I-it's very hard to think

46:22.906 --> 46:25.776 align:middle
about all the different
possible ramifications.

46:25.776 --> 46:27.536 align:middle
And I think the answer
is, basically,

46:27.536 --> 46:30.396 align:middle
I don't think there's likely
to be any important interaction

46:30.396 --> 46:33.576 align:middle
between the two, because I
believe Congress will wind

46:33.576 --> 46:36.906 align:middle
up acting, and-as it will
and must, in the end,

46:36.906 --> 46:40.876 align:middle
to raise the debt ceiling in a
way that doesn't risk, you know,

46:40.876 --> 46:42.846 align:middle
the progress we're
making against inflation

46:42.846 --> 46:44.536 align:middle
and the economy and
the financial sector.

46:44.536 --> 46:45.796 align:middle
I believe that that will happen.

46:45.796 --> 46:46.676 align:middle
I believe it will happen.

46:47.246 --> 46:48.536 align:middle
You know, we, of course,

46:48.536 --> 46:52.706 align:middle
will monitor money market
conditions carefully as-you

46:52.816 --> 46:55.616 align:middle
know, as the process moves on.

46:56.036 --> 46:59.046 align:middle
For example, the Treasury
General Account will shrink

46:59.086 --> 47:00.506 align:middle
down, and then it
will grow back up.

47:00.506 --> 47:03.486 align:middle
And we understand there'll be
lots of flows between there

47:03.486 --> 47:06.146 align:middle
and the overnight repo
facility and reserves.

47:06.526 --> 47:07.636 align:middle
We understand all that.

47:07.636 --> 47:09.296 align:middle
We're watching it carefully.

47:09.296 --> 47:13.636 align:middle
We'll just be monitoring it.

47:15.746 --> 47:32.926 align:middle
Thank you very much.

